f later if scare card comes.  \n2. **Small raise (e.g., 100)** → Could take down tiny pot now if opponent folds weak hands, but my hand is so weak I can’t withstand a re-raise, and opponent may call with any pair.  \n3. **Fold (-1)** → Too conservative given pot odds; I’ve invested little, getting a free card.  \n\nFolding is too weak, raising is spewy with no semi-bluff equity (only 4 outs to a pair that may still lose, plus 2 outs to a gutshot—6 total weak outs).  \n\nChecking seems correct to realize equity.  \n\n---\n\n**Final choice:** Check and see river.  \n\n---\n\nEvaluation: Weak hand with minimal draws, opponent checked, pot small, taking free card is best.  \nReason: No value in betting with near-zero equity; free river may improve to a pair or straight draw to bluff later.  \nAnswer: 0'}
